        "The browser never sees TypeScript. Eventually, it all becomes JavaScript."
      ]
    },
    cheaperBugs: {
      heading: "Bugs are cheaper before they exist",
      paragraphs: [
        "One of the biggest advantages of TypeScript isn't writing code. It's avoiding bad code.",
        "Imagine a simple function that calculates totals. If someone passes a string instead of a number, JavaScript fails at runtime.",
        "With TypeScript, your editor highlights the mistake immediately—often before you even save the file."
      ],
      badCallLabel: "Argument type mismatch warning in IDE:"
    },
    typesAreDoc: {
      heading: "Types are documentation",
      paragraphs: [
        "One thing I didn't expect was how much easier code becomes to read.",
        "When a function parameter is typed with an interface, you instantly know what fields are expected without reading the implementation.",
        "JavaScript leaves you guessing what 'data' contains. TypeScript documents it automatically."
      ]
    },
    interfaces: {
      heading: "Interfaces became my favorite feature",
      paragraphs: [
        "One concept that completely changed how I organize projects was interfaces.",
        "Instead of remembering object structures, I define them once. Now every part of the application agrees on what a User looks like. Frontend, Backend, APIs... everyone speaks the same language."
      ]
    },
    generics: {
      heading: "Generics looked terrifying...",
      paragraphs: [
        "...until they didn't.",
        "The first time I saw generic type variables (<T>), it looked extremely complex. Months later, Generics became one of my favorite features.",
        "Instead of writing multiple functions, one function can safely work with different data types while preserving type information. That's incredibly powerful."
      ]
    },
    inference: {
      heading: "Type inference is underrated",
      paragraphs: [
        "One thing I appreciate about TypeScript is that it doesn't force you to type everything.",
        "Often, it already knows. If you declare a string, it infers the type automatically. The compiler does a lot of the work for you."
      ]
    },
    refactoring: {
      heading: "Refactoring becomes less scary",
      paragraphs: [
        "One thing I noticed after using TypeScript for a while... I became less afraid of changing code.",
        "Rename a property, move a function, or split a module, and the compiler points out everything that needs to be updated. It's like having another developer reviewing my work in real time."
      ]
    },
